play attaches an IQ recording (a .cf32 file: the raw samples a radio
produced, the kind the daemon writes and the fixtures directory contains) as
a pretend radio, then tunes on it exactly as 'tune' would, so every other
command works the same: ley set adjusts it, ley spectrum shows it.

The frequency defaults to the file's centre (from the .json sidecar beside
the file: center_hz plus the first 'expect' offset when present) and the
mode to the first 'expect' entry's mode; --mode and --freq override. The
pretend radio is removed on exit unless --persistent is given, in which
case the channel and the device outlive the command; 'ley stop' removes the
channel and 'ley devices detach <id>' the pretend radio. No hardware is
needed.

A recording 'ley record' made is named by its id or its ley://recordings/
URI. An IQ recording is tuned as above; an audio one is played by the daemon
through its own speakers, and on a terminal space pauses and resumes it.

--over plays one over of a gated audio recording's part: the over's number in
the part (1 is the first time the squelch opened), from half a second before it
to half a second after it closed, as the speech decoder heard it. --from and --to play a stretch of the part instead, in capture samples on
the timeline 'ley recordings show --json' and the part's sidecar give.

Usage:
  ley play <file.cf32> [flags]

Examples:
  ley play fixtures/nfm_tone.cf32              # decode a fixture and listen
  ley play recording.cf32 --loop               # keep playing until Ctrl-C
  ley play recording.cf32 --freq 146.52 --mode nfm
  ley play recording.cf32 --persistent --json  # leave it running, print ids
  ley play job_01J... --part 2 --over 3        # the third over of part 2

Flags:
      --bw string        how wide a slice of spectrum to listen to: a bare number is kHz (12.5), or 200k, 12500 (default: the mode's usual width)
      --freq string      frequency to listen to within the recording; a bare number is MHz, e.g. 146.52 (default: the file's centre plus its first expect offset)
      --from uint        play the part from this capture sample on (default: its start)
      --gain string      receiver gain: auto, dB such as 30 for the first stage, or stages such as LNA=0,VGA=20 (ley help gain); default: leave the radio's setting
  -h, --help             help for play
      --loop             start over when the file ends, until Ctrl-C (default: stop at the end)
      --mode string      how to decode: nfm (two-way voice), wfm (broadcast), am (airband), usb, lsb, cw, raw; fm or ssb pick by frequency (default: by band; ley help modes)
      --no-audio         decode but do not play through the speakers (use with --persistent or --json)
      --over int         play one over of the part, by its number in the part, e.g. 3 (default: the whole part)
      --part int         which part of a ley://recordings/ recording to play, e.g. 2 (default: the first)
      --persistent       leave the channel running after the command exits and print its ids (for scripts)
      --squelch string   mute the audio when the signal is weaker than this level: auto (default for voice modes), off, or a level like -40 (dBFS; 0 is the loudest possible)
      --to uint          play the part up to this capture sample (default: its end)
      --volume string    speaker volume: 0 to 1, or a percentage like 50% (default "1")

Global Flags:
      --ascii           draw with ASCII only (no box, bar or tree glyphs)
      --color string    colour output: auto, always or never (default "auto")
      --json            machine output: proto3 JSON (NDJSON for streams)
      --socket string   daemon socket path (default: user daemon UDS, $LEYLINE_SOCKET)
